-- Example: schedule the pricing watchdog.
--
-- Pure SQL, no edge function in the way: the function lives in the database
-- (99_supabase_migration_pricing_watchdog_v1.sql) and cron calls it
-- directly, so it still speaks when a function fails to start, a cron secret
-- is wrong, or every 5-minute call is refused. Every 10 minutes it looks at
-- each live or simulating hotel that is entitled, not a test hotel, and
-- connected to a property system, and posts to the Slack alert webhook when
--   - no pricing run has finished for the hotel in 30 minutes (behind), or
--   - it is 2 hours or more into the hotel's own day and today's daily pass
--     has not finished (pass_late).
-- A live hotel is critical, a simulating one is warn (not sent unless the
-- second argument is 'warn'). One line per hotel while it is stuck (no
-- repeat within 6 hours), one recovery line when it is fine again, and
-- nothing while the sync's own alert for the hotel (reads failing, pricing
-- failing, disconnected) is out.
--
-- Prereqs: extensions pg_cron and pg_net; the webhook in Vault.
--
-- 0) Ensure the extensions exist:
--    create extension if not exists pg_cron;
--    create extension if not exists pg_net;
--
-- 1) Store the alert webhook in Vault, under this exact name. It is the same
--    address as the MAYA_ALERT_WEBHOOK function secret (stored twice on
--    purpose: SQL cannot read the function secrets). Without it the watchdog
--    finds nothing to post to, says so on Pilot health ("Alerts: missing.
--    pricing-watchdog said ..."), and records no alert.
--    select vault.create_secret('https://hooks.slack.com/services/...', 'maya_alert_webhook');
--    -- To change it later:
--    -- select vault.update_secret((select id from vault.secrets where name = 'maya_alert_webhook'), 'https://...');
--
-- 2) Schedule (every 10 minutes, at :04, :14, ... so it lands after a tick):
--    select cron.schedule(
--      'pricing-watchdog-every-10-min',
--      '4-59/10 * * * *',
--      $$ select public.pricing_watchdog(true, 'critical'); $$
--    );
--    -- To hear about simulating hotels too (as warnings):
--    -- $$ select public.pricing_watchdog(true, 'warn'); $$
--
-- Inspect:    select jobid, jobname, schedule, active from cron.job;
-- Runs log:   select * from cron.job_run_details order by start_time desc limit 20;
-- Unschedule: select cron.unschedule('pricing-watchdog-every-10-min');
--
-- What it would say right now, without posting or recording anything:
--   select name, mode, behind, pass_late, minutes_since_run, pass_date, hours_into_day,
--          failed_runs, reads_failing, action, detail
--     from public.pricing_watchdog(false);
--
-- What it has posted (the same rows raiseAlert writes, key pricing-watchdog:<hotel id>):
--   select event_type, entity_id, detail, created_at
--     from public.platform_audit_events
--    where entity_id like 'pricing-watchdog:%'
--    order by created_at desc limit 20;
--
-- Whether pg_net delivered the last posts (responses are kept about 6 hours):
--   select id, status_code, error_msg, created from net._http_response order by id desc limit 10;
